1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,491
A long spanner loosens a stuck nut.

2
00:00:02,491 --> 00:00:04,321
A pedal turns on a long crank.

3
00:00:04,321 --> 00:00:05,974
A door opens at its handle.

4
00:00:06,374 --> 00:00:07,850
Three different pushes.

5
00:00:07,850 --> 00:00:08,906
One same idea.

6
00:00:09,356 --> 00:00:13,264
In the college corridor, Aarav
pushes a heavy door near its hinges.

7
00:00:13,264 --> 00:00:14,351
It barely moves.

8
00:00:14,701 --> 00:00:17,200
He pushes just as hard at the handle.

9
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:18,343
It swings open.

10
00:00:18,343 --> 00:00:19,243
Same push.

11
00:00:19,186 --> 00:00:20,929
Why does the spot matter?

12
00:00:21,429 --> 00:00:22,698
The answer is torque.

13
00:00:22,698 --> 00:00:25,673
Soon you will know why handles
sit far from the hinge.

14
00:00:26,273 --> 00:00:27,837
A force makes a thing move.

15
00:00:27,837 --> 00:00:29,313
Torque makes a thing turn.

16
00:00:29,663 --> 00:00:34,127
Torque is the turning effect of a
force about an axis, the line a body

17
00:00:34,127 --> 00:00:35,127
turns around.

18
00:00:35,477 --> 00:00:37,825
For a door, the axis is the hinge.

19
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:39,356
For a nut, the bolt.

20
00:00:39,356 --> 00:00:40,980
For a pedal, the axle.

21
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:43,294
Torque depends on three things.

22
00:00:43,294 --> 00:00:44,561
How hard you push.

23
00:00:44,561 --> 00:00:46,065
How far from the axis.

24
00:00:46,065 --> 00:00:47,332
And at what angle.

25
00:00:47,932 --> 00:00:50,142
Let's test it on our motion notebook.

26
00:00:50,142 --> 00:00:52,021
Aarav's door, seen from above.

27
00:00:52,021 --> 00:00:53,685
The hinge is on the left.

28
00:00:54,035 --> 00:00:57,558
Every push is the same: twenty
newtons, at right angles.

29
00:00:57,958 --> 00:01:00,918
Push at zero point three
metres from the hinge.

30
00:01:00,918 --> 00:01:03,001
The torque is six newton metres.

31
00:01:03,001 --> 00:01:04,651
The door turns a little.

32
00:01:05,051 --> 00:01:07,142
Push at zero point six metres.

33
00:01:07,142 --> 00:01:09,383
Twice as far, twice the torque.

34
00:01:09,383 --> 00:01:10,850
Twelve newton metres.

35
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:14,075
Push at the handle,
zero point nine metres.

36
00:01:14,075 --> 00:01:15,573
Eighteen newton metres.

37
00:01:15,573 --> 00:01:17,279
The door swings the most.

38
00:01:17,779 --> 00:01:21,231
On the graph, torque grows in
a straight line with distance.

39
00:01:21,831 --> 00:01:22,791
Slow-mo replay.

40
00:01:22,791 --> 00:01:24,084
Freeze at the push.

41
00:01:24,434 --> 00:01:28,235
The arrow from the hinge to the
hand is r, the position vector.

42
00:01:28,235 --> 00:01:30,535
The push F is at right angles to it.

43
00:01:30,935 --> 00:01:34,725
Now push at the handle at a
slant, partly toward the hinge.

44
00:01:34,725 --> 00:01:38,421
The angle between r and F is
one hundred and fifty degrees.

45
00:01:38,821 --> 00:01:41,311
The torque drops to nine newton metres.

46
00:01:41,311 --> 00:01:44,105
Only part of a slanted
push turns the door.

47
00:01:44,705 --> 00:01:47,506
Let's build the formula,
one step at a time.

48
00:01:47,856 --> 00:01:48,756
Step one.

49
00:01:48,647 --> 00:01:50,036
Torque is a vector.

50
00:01:50,036 --> 00:01:51,338
It is r cross F.

51
00:01:52,088 --> 00:01:52,988
Step two.

52
00:01:52,936 --> 00:01:57,861
Its size is r F sine theta, where
theta is the angle between r and F.

53
00:01:58,611 --> 00:01:59,511
Step three.

54
00:01:59,485 --> 00:02:03,752
r sine theta is the moment arm: the
shortest distance from the axis to

55
00:02:03,752 --> 00:02:05,320
the line of the force.

56
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:07,724
So torque is the moment arm times F.

57
00:02:08,474 --> 00:02:09,374
Step four.

58
00:02:09,221 --> 00:02:10,988
The unit is the newton metre.

59
00:02:10,988 --> 00:02:13,912
The dimensions are ML
squared T to the minus two.

60
00:02:14,662 --> 00:02:17,453
The direction comes from
the right hand rule.

61
00:02:17,453 --> 00:02:19,485
Curl your fingers from r to F.

62
00:02:19,485 --> 00:02:22,518
Your thumb gives the
torque, out of the screen.

63
00:02:23,118 --> 00:02:24,154
Remember this.

64
00:02:24,154 --> 00:02:28,490
Torque is the force times the
perpendicular distance from the axis to its

65
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:29,390
line.

66
00:02:29,732 --> 00:02:32,477
So why is the handle far from the hinge?

67
00:02:32,477 --> 00:02:33,539
Link by link.

68
00:02:33,889 --> 00:02:36,570
Your push is about the same every time.

69
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:39,981
But the handle is far,
so the moment arm is big.

70
00:02:40,381 --> 00:02:42,427
A big arm gives a big torque.

71
00:02:42,427 --> 00:02:44,164
So the door turns easily.

72
00:02:44,764 --> 00:02:45,859
Flip it around.

73
00:02:45,859 --> 00:02:50,059
For the same nine newton metres,
at the handle you need ten newtons.

74
00:02:50,059 --> 00:02:51,237
Halfway, twenty.

75
00:02:51,237 --> 00:02:52,804
Near the hinge, sixty.

76
00:02:53,404 --> 00:02:54,385
Now you try.

77
00:02:54,385 --> 00:02:58,695
Which turns a stuck bolt more: forty
newtons at zero point two metres,

78
00:02:58,695 --> 00:03:01,093
or twenty newtons at zero point five?

79
00:03:01,093 --> 00:03:02,311
Pause and decide.

80
00:03:05,411 --> 00:03:07,276
Twenty at zero point five!

81
00:03:07,276 --> 00:03:09,292
That gives ten newton metres.

82
00:03:09,292 --> 00:03:12,095
Forty at zero point two gives only eight.

83
00:03:12,745 --> 00:03:13,787
Side by side.

84
00:03:13,787 --> 00:03:17,964
Push at right angles, and the
moment arm is the full distance r.

85
00:03:18,364 --> 00:03:22,774
Push at a slant, and the line of the
force passes closer to the hinge.

86
00:03:22,774 --> 00:03:24,491
The moment arm is shorter.

87
00:03:25,091 --> 00:03:25,991
Your turn.

88
00:03:25,886 --> 00:03:30,155
Aarav pushes very hard, but straight
along the door, toward the hinge.

89
00:03:30,155 --> 00:03:31,142
Will it turn?

90
00:03:31,142 --> 00:03:32,311
Pause and decide.

91
00:03:35,511 --> 00:03:36,411
No!

92
00:03:35,933 --> 00:03:38,807
The line of the force
passes through the hinge.

93
00:03:38,807 --> 00:03:41,783
The moment arm is zero,
so the torque is zero.

94
00:03:42,433 --> 00:03:44,089
Now, one worked example.

95
00:03:44,089 --> 00:03:45,458
Aarav's bicycle pedal.

96
00:03:45,808 --> 00:03:48,399
The crank is zero point
one seven metres long.

97
00:03:48,399 --> 00:03:51,066
He pushes straight down
with two hundred newtons.

98
00:03:51,566 --> 00:03:54,942
With the crank level, the
push is at right angles.

99
00:03:54,942 --> 00:03:58,111
Torque is zero point one
seven times two hundred.

100
00:03:58,111 --> 00:03:59,863
Thirty four newton metres.

101
00:04:00,613 --> 00:04:03,969
Lower down, the crank is thirty
degrees from the vertical.

102
00:04:03,969 --> 00:04:07,333
Now theta is thirty degrees,
and sine thirty is one half.

103
00:04:08,083 --> 00:04:10,493
So the torque is seventeen newton metres.

104
00:04:10,493 --> 00:04:11,450
Half as much.

105
00:04:11,450 --> 00:04:14,471
That is why a push does
so little near the bottom.

106
00:04:15,121 --> 00:04:16,586
Now, the exam trap.

107
00:04:16,936 --> 00:04:20,918
Many students write torque as F times
r, the distance to the point where

108
00:04:20,918 --> 00:04:21,942
the force acts.

109
00:04:22,342 --> 00:04:24,562
That works only at right angles.

110
00:04:24,562 --> 00:04:28,911
Torque is F times the perpendicular
distance from the axis to the line

111
00:04:28,911 --> 00:04:29,996
of the force.

112
00:04:30,646 --> 00:04:31,943
Where is this asked?

113
00:04:31,943 --> 00:04:36,077
In torque as r cross F, in moment arm
questions, and in the direction of

114
00:04:36,077 --> 00:04:36,977
torque.

115
00:04:37,251 --> 00:04:38,422
Exam level now.

116
00:04:38,422 --> 00:04:43,135
A ten newton force acts along the line
y equals x plus two, with distances

117
00:04:43,135 --> 00:04:44,035
in metres.

118
00:04:43,999 --> 00:04:47,015
Find the size of its
torque about the origin.

119
00:04:47,015 --> 00:04:48,236
Pause and solve.

120
00:04:53,636 --> 00:04:54,988
The answer is B.

121
00:04:54,988 --> 00:04:56,979
Ten root two newton metres.

122
00:04:57,479 --> 00:05:01,649
The line makes forty five degrees with
the x axis, and crosses the y axis

123
00:05:01,649 --> 00:05:02,549
at two.

124
00:05:02,788 --> 00:05:06,997
So the moment arm from the origin
is two times sine forty five.

125
00:05:06,997 --> 00:05:08,235
Root two metres.

126
00:05:08,735 --> 00:05:10,673
Torque is ten times root two.

127
00:05:10,673 --> 00:05:13,080
About fourteen point one newton metres.

128
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:15,221
Here is your recap.

129
00:05:15,221 --> 00:05:16,805
Screenshot this card.

130
00:05:20,005 --> 00:05:21,943
Can you answer these without help?

131
00:05:22,343 --> 00:05:23,243
One.

132
00:05:22,732 --> 00:05:24,972
Why do door handles
sit far from the hinge?

133
00:05:25,873 --> 00:05:26,773
Two.

134
00:05:26,257 --> 00:05:29,171
Why does a push straight toward
the hinge not turn a door?

135
00:05:30,071 --> 00:05:30,971
Three.

136
00:05:30,570 --> 00:05:33,394
How is the moment arm
different from the distance r?

137
00:05:34,293 --> 00:05:38,955
Next time: how can a ball moving in a
straight line have angular momentum?
